- op: discuss  # 2e5f
  at: '2026-07-31T07:53:20Z'  # 2e5f
  by: agent  # 2e5f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 2e5f
  clock: 4  # 2e5f
  nonce: 2e5f  # 2e5f
  message: "CLOSING as done. PR #116, merged, dev 729c853098. The mypy ratchet BLOCKS merges as of 2026-07-31.\n\nWHAT THE ITEM ASKED FOR: \"Phase 1: report-only. Phase 2 (after W4 clean): blocking.\" Phase 1 shipped long ago; Phase 2 is now live. The \"after clean\" precondition was retired rather than met — see below.\n\nTHE SEQUENCING PRECONDITION WAS NEVER REAL. check-mypy-ratchet fails only when an error code GROWS against the baseline, never on the absolute count, so 1080 errors and 0 errors gate identically. --mode=strict exited 0 on dev before the flip and after it. The documented ramp (\"grind to zero, THEN flip\") reads as a hard dependency across the plan and the notebooks and is not one. The cost of waiting is on the record: WI-basat existed because seven errors accumulated silently over ten days -- not because anyone ignored a failure, but because a non-blocking warning is by definition a signal nobody must act on.\n\nTHE ONE OPEN RISK WAS A DEFECT, NOT A REASON TO WAIT -- AND IT WAS ALREADY LIVE. This item held the flip on: \"a mypy release could recategorise errors and block every PR at once, independent of any code change.\" Investigating that found something older and worse. The baseline recorded `total` and `by_code` but NOT the mypy version that produced them, while CI installed `mypy~=2.1`, which admits 2.2 and 2.3. A shrink-only baseline whose measuring instrument can change under it was never a ratchet at all: in warning mode a release could have moved every number with no code change and nobody could have told tooling drift from code drift.\n\nThen this PR's own CI proved it was not hypothetical: the fresh install resolved **mypy 2.3.0** while the committed baseline had been measured locally at **2.1.0**. The drift was already in effect, silently, between any clean install and a developer machine.\n\nClosed in three parts rather than accepted:\n1. the baseline records `mypy_version`;\n2. the ratchet REFUSES to compare counts across versions, emitting a distinct INSTRUMENT MISMATCH diagnostic instead of reporting six unrelated codes as \"grown\" (which is both wrong and the most confusing possible way to fail). It blocks EVEN WHEN COUNTS SHRANK -- a shrink measured by a different instrument is not evidence of progress, and banking it would ratify tooling drift as a drain;\n3. the pin tightens to ~=2.1.0 in the CI job AND in both packages' dev extras, so one series is used everywhere and adopting a newer mypy is a deliberate change that must carry its own --update-baseline.\n\nBackward compatible: a baseline with no recorded version compares as before rather than hard-failing, so no flag day.\n\nTHE FLIP IS FOUR EDITS AND THREE ARE INDIVIDUALLY SILENT. This nearly shipped as a half-flip. Removing `continue-on-error` makes the job red and gates NOTHING on its own, because the merge verdict is `ci-complete` and a job absent from its `needs` cannot fail it -- which mypy was, by design, as the job's own comment stated. Being in `needs` is still insufficient: ci-complete runs `if: always()` and decides by explicitly testing each result, so a job can sit in `needs` and be ignored by the verdict. All four edits are now made, and tests/test_check_mypy_ratchet_blocking.py pins all four plus the pin and the baseline's recorded instrument, because a half-flip is indistinguishable from a flip from the outside. Two revert paths mutation-tested.\n\nDISCLOSED GAP: ci.yml is not a mapped source, so editing IT selects no tests (the INV-kinin class). A silent revert of this flip is caught by the twice-daily full suite, not per-PR.\n\nBEARING ON INV-zogud: the gate MECHANISM is now complete -- surface measured, ratcheted, instrument-pinned, and blocking. What remains for INV-zogud is drainage of the 1080-error surface, which is per-site semantic work and unaffected by this item. The item's own framing (\"after W4 clean\") is retired: blocking did not need the drain, and the drain does not need blocking, but the drain is now protected against regrowth while it happens."  # 2e5f
